  0f2e134a-f24a-4773-8c1c-db2322eb5239:
    id: 0f2e134a-f24a-4773-8c1c-db2322eb5239
    content: As a user building multi-phase AI workflows (e.g. refinement, TDD, dev, refactor, QA), I need to organize sub-workflows within a parent workflow
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: pending
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  506eafb9-e61f-4dc2-8561-b70ca27f9d5f:
    id: 506eafb9-e61f-4dc2-8561-b70ca27f9d5f
    content: Currently workflows are flat — a workflow has steps but steps cannot themselves be workflows
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: pending
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  f872161d-3ee7-4a77-ad6f-48b38d13987e:
    id: f872161d-3ee7-4a77-ad6f-48b38d13987e
    content: The guards `hasAncestorWorkflow()` and `hasDescendantWorkflow()` in workflowHelpers.ts explicitly block nesting
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: pending
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  dfbe70d7-0a91-4d25-b730-6d913981659b:
    id: dfbe70d7-0a91-4d25-b730-6d913981659b
    content: This prevents expressing naturally hierarchical processes where a phase (e.g. "dev") has its own sequential sub-steps that must all complete before the next phase begins
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: pending
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
