  8309fdd2-67c2-41c1-9367-25063229901c:
    id: 8309fdd2-67c2-41c1-9367-25063229901c
    content: Recurse is most useful when a prior step produces multiple outputs (via US2 multi-node acceptance) but is not dependent on it — it works with any nodes that happen to be in a step
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  75824f4b-c213-4e1a-bac5-b7a804aee581:
    id: 75824f4b-c213-4e1a-bac5-b7a804aee581
    content: The existing workflow execution state tracking handles one running item per terminal — recurse initiates a new workflow run after the current one completes, it does not run items in parallel
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  4fa2edc1-6636-4c26-b90d-e1b6fde4d94b:
    id: 4fa2edc1-6636-4c26-b90d-e1b6fde4d94b
    content: Runaway recursion risk exists if a workflow creates output that feeds back into itself — the existing provenance circularity check (US3) and the natural stop at non-autonomous steps mitigate this, but a maximum recursion depth or node count limit should be considered as a safety net
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
