  b8c05695-ea0f-43e3-af48-39300f9eeaa0:
    id: b8c05695-ea0f-43e3-af48-39300f9eeaa0
    content: Risks and edge cases
    children:
      - 2ab20fb0-c893-442f-989d-e9b70a046c83
      - cf3190d3-e5bf-4799-bd49-be5d3fde4213
      - 9c8e7299-a137-46c1-84e9-0463b2461d7a
      - 141f3ff2-336b-4223-936b-0a6d20ffa2a6
      - f52998f3-511b-4258-b829-c34116e1ac20
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  2ab20fb0-c893-442f-989d-e9b70a046c83:
    id: 2ab20fb0-c893-442f-989d-e9b70a046c83
    content: 'Timing: the recurse check must run after the current node has fully advanced and the terminal is available. The 1s delay before sendContentToTerminal helps, but the recurse-triggered startWorkflow also needs a delay to avoid racing with the previous node''s cleanup.'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  cf3190d3-e5bf-4799-bd49-be5d3fde4213:
    id: cf3190d3-e5bf-4799-bd49-be5d3fde4213
    content: 'Terminal availability: if the terminal is somehow still assigned to the previous node when recurse tries to start the next, the startWorkflow guard will reject it. This is safe but means the recurse silently fails. A retry or better timing would be needed.'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  9c8e7299-a137-46c1-84e9-0463b2461d7a:
    id: 9c8e7299-a137-46c1-84e9-0463b2461d7a
    content: 'Step with both recurse and decomposition: if a step has decomposition enabled and also recurse, the decomposition creates multiple nodes at that step, and then recurse would process them one at a time through the remaining chain. This is the intended use case and should work naturally.'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  141f3ff2-336b-4223-936b-0a6d20ffa2a6:
    id: 141f3ff2-336b-4223-936b-0a6d20ffa2a6
    content: 'NeedsReview interaction: if a node triggers NeedsReview at a recurse step, the step pauses for review. When the user continues, the node advances. The recurse check should trigger after the continuation-triggered advancement, not after the original NeedsReview pause.'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  f52998f3-511b-4258-b829-c34116e1ac20:
    id: f52998f3-511b-4258-b829-c34116e1ac20
    content: 'Workflow with only one step: recurse on a single-step workflow would complete the node and then try to find the next waiting node in the same step. This works if multiple items were placed there.'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
