  251bd2df-31f1-46cf-919f-1dfbca5a5374:
    id: 251bd2df-31f1-46cf-919f-1dfbca5a5374
    content: 'Root cause is unclear — the code path exists and looks correct: `notifyWorkflowEvent` → `isWindowFocused` IPC → skip if focused, else `showNotification` IPC → main creates `Notification({title, body})` and calls `.show()`. Bundle id is `com.arborescent.app`, preferences default `desktopNotifications: true`. The bug is more likely operational (focus tracking, OS permission, delivery channel) than missing code.'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  26eeecad-11ac-48a8-bb7a-ee9eaa8fc9dc:
    id: 26eeecad-11ac-48a8-bb7a-ee9eaa8fc9dc
    content: Focus tracking is cached, not queried. `notificationHandlers.ts` maintains a `windowFocused` module-level variable updated by `focus`/`blur` events. If those events fail to fire for any reason (initial launch state, certain window-management operations, dev-tools interactions, Mac Spaces/desktop switching) the cache desynchronises. The renderer then sees `focused=true` while the user sees the app unfocused, and the notification short-circuits at line 10 of workflowNotification.ts. Prime suspect for "no notifications ever" if the cache starts out wrong and never gets corrected.
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  a68267c9-9589-423b-95bd-9a799078aa23:
    id: a68267c9-9589-423b-95bd-9a799078aa23
    content: 'macOS Notification Center quirks: notifications require the app''s bundle id to be registered with Notification Center. On dev builds launched via `npm start`, Electron uses a generic bundle, so the app won''t appear in System Settings → Notifications → Arborescent. On packaged builds the bundle is right, but if the user ever denied the initial permission prompt (or it was auto-dismissed during a build-install dance), System Settings will silently suppress notifications with no visible error in the app.'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: pending
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  7f4b70a9-805c-48e0-a613-936ec99f3bc4:
    id: 7f4b70a9-805c-48e0-a613-936ec99f3bc4
    content: Linux depends on a notification daemon (libnotify + dunst/mako/gnome-shell). `Notification.isSupported()` returns true if libnotify is present but that does not mean a daemon is actually running or listening. Silent failures are common. "Same on Linux" may share root cause with macOS (focus tracking) or may be platform-specific (no daemon) — have to treat as potentially two bugs until instrumented.
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: pending
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  ecc2a127-087f-46f8-9dfa-4ed7dc84f44b:
    id: ecc2a127-087f-46f8-9dfa-4ed7dc84f44b
    content: Do Not Disturb / Focus modes on macOS silently suppress notifications — not a code bug but an easy false negative while debugging. Worth explicitly checking before spending time on code changes.
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: pending
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  289a1872-6520-46f7-a9a9-6ff8da0f575b:
    id: 289a1872-6520-46f7-a9a9-6ff8da0f575b
    content: Observability is thin. The log line at notificationHandlers.ts line 25 ("Showing notification") prints when `.show()` is called, but the surrounding evidence (did IPC arrive? did Notification.isSupported() return true?) is only partially logged. Hard to tell from current logs where on the pipeline the failure happens.
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
