      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  8b0ae074-1691-4e3e-a860-14ba792a5828:
    id: 8b0ae074-1691-4e3e-a860-14ba792a5828
    content: Context — Feedback currently regenerates UUIDs on every non-parent node and gives the user no visual cue for what the model proposed to change. The user loses stable identity on nodes that the model returned untouched (breaking selection, expansion state, links, and producing noisy `.arbo` diffs) and has to scan the whole subtree mentally to spot edits. Both problems share the same missing capability — a structural diff between the prior subtree and the feedback output — so a single LCS-based diff layer should drive both UUID preservation and the in-panel highlight.
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  0e0d9f15-3eae-418c-8058-0223f7e7dc32:
    id: 0e0d9f15-3eae-418c-8058-0223f7e7dc32
    content: User — Brian, the sole user of Arborescent, running the feedback workflow on a node with children
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  08fb1728-cf7f-4a60-8480-1bce13db33d1:
    id: 08fb1728-cf7f-4a60-8480-1bce13db33d1
    content: Current experience — After feedback runs, all non-parent UUIDs are replaced regardless of content and the panel shows the proposed subtree with no indication of what changed; the user has to compare against the prior tree from memory before accepting
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  c5198b43-5322-4be0-b63c-e0c48740bdaa:
    id: c5198b43-5322-4be0-b63c-e0c48740bdaa
    content: Goal — When the feedback panel opens, classify every node in the proposed subtree against the prior subtree, retain the existing UUID for any node that maps to a prior one (changed or not), and visually highlight modifications, additions, and removals in the panel so the user can see the diff at a glance before accepting
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  da138d44-b2db-4d5d-8adf-fd7cf62f9998:
    id: da138d44-b2db-4d5d-8adf-fd7cf62f9998
    content: Business rules
    children:
      - efc19d85-4de1-4eba-98b8-756ca9b97e42
      - 01924a56-83db-4359-9c79-267621333c78
      - e3ea0ea4-1598-48df-ab8f-2e21f9cfd8c0
      - e31aff17-810f-4ba4-ac95-0ad57ade47ff
      - 043ecd4a-6f2c-4fd4-beaf-891e0fcd842a
      - a8c2f1c2-52dd-42a3-949e-5b2cda923c56
      - 8b4d77b6-92b2-4a2d-824d-357f437d15c5
      - 5d7fbc5c-1e0b-419a-8e54-7ad0e536b084
      - 81c1641b-4f29-4151-ab3f-1ead7c8f9881
      - 1fd5efd3-8e5f-4286-ad19-cf193a1b78bb
      - 097542e8-c71a-46cf-86db-628ca1ad2ce9
      - c6c71aab-c43c-4ada-be40-ab532c1fb6e0
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  efc19d85-4de1-4eba-98b8-756ca9b97e42:
    id: efc19d85-4de1-4eba-98b8-756ca9b97e42
    content: 1. The parent node retains its existing UUID under all modes (no regression).
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  01924a56-83db-4359-9c79-267621333c78:
    id: 01924a56-83db-4359-9c79-267621333c78
    content: 2. In normal feedback mode, every non-parent node in the proposed subtree is classified against the prior subtree as unchanged, modified, or added; every prior node that no longer maps to anything in the output is classified as removed.
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  e3ea0ea4-1598-48df-ab8f-2e21f9cfd8c0:
    id: e3ea0ea4-1598-48df-ab8f-2e21f9cfd8c0
    content: '3. Classification uses LCS (Myers) over content equality at each sibling level under a matched parent: equal nodes form unchanged anchors; the gaps between anchors are then matched by relative position to identify modified pairs, leftover insertions, and leftover deletions.'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  e31aff17-810f-4ba4-ac95-0ad57ade47ff:
    id: e31aff17-810f-4ba4-ac95-0ad57ade47ff
    content: 4. Unchanged and modified nodes both inherit the existing UUID from their prior counterpart — UUID is identity, not a content hash — and only added nodes receive freshly generated UUIDs.
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  043ecd4a-6f2c-4fd4-beaf-891e0fcd842a:
    id: 043ecd4a-6f2c-4fd4-beaf-891e0fcd842a
    content: '5. In decomposition mode the reconciliation is skipped entirely: every output node is treated as added and receives a fresh UUID, no removed list is shown, and no UUIDs are reused.'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  a8c2f1c2-52dd-42a3-949e-5b2cda923c56:
    id: a8c2f1c2-52dd-42a3-949e-5b2cda923c56
    content: 6. The diff runs once when the feedback panel opens. While the panel is open, in-panel text edits trigger only a cheap per-node comparison on edit commit (blur or explicit save) — flipping the affected node between modified and unchanged — and structural edits in the panel (inserting, deleting, or reordering a node) trigger an LCS re-run for that single sibling level only. Nothing re-runs per keystroke.
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: pending
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  8b4d77b6-92b2-4a2d-824d-357f437d15c5:
    id: 8b4d77b6-92b2-4a2d-824d-357f437d15c5
    content: 7. Classification flips and added/removed transitions in the panel use a brief CSS background transition (no jolt) so the user is not perturbed by mid-edit changes.
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  5d7fbc5c-1e0b-419a-8e54-7ad0e536b084:
    id: 5d7fbc5c-1e0b-419a-8e54-7ad0e536b084
    content: 8. Highlights and the removed-node indicator live entirely inside the feedback panel and are never rendered in the main tree. They are persisted as part of the saved feedback panel record so a save-and-reload reopens the panel with the same visual state, but they never attach to the underlying tree nodes themselves.
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  81c1641b-4f29-4151-ab3f-1ead7c8f9881:
    id: 81c1641b-4f29-4151-ab3f-1ead7c8f9881
    content: 9. Modified nodes show a blue background, added nodes show a green background, and removed nodes show a red background with strikethrough in their original position within the panel.
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  1fd5efd3-8e5f-4286-ad19-cf193a1b78bb:
    id: 1fd5efd3-8e5f-4286-ad19-cf193a1b78bb
    content: 10. The modified-node blue must be visibly distinct from the panel's selection and focus styling so the two states cannot be confused.
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  097542e8-c71a-46cf-86db-628ca1ad2ce9:
    id: 097542e8-c71a-46cf-86db-628ca1ad2ce9
    content: 11. Accepting the panel commits the merged subtree (with UUIDs and content already resolved) atomically and discards the persisted panel record and its highlight state; rejecting discards both the merged subtree and the panel record.
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  c6c71aab-c43c-4ada-be40-ab532c1fb6e0:
    id: c6c71aab-c43c-4ada-be40-ab532c1fb6e0
    content: 12. When duplicate-content siblings appear, relative position within an LCS gap drives matching so results are deterministic across repeated runs that produce the same output.
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  a3a9f191-150e-44e9-bda9-e926426739b1:
