  0b6309aa-226e-4987-858d-6f0b229011af:
    id: 0b6309aa-226e-4987-858d-6f0b229011af
    content: Reviewer framed `fit()` → `scrollToBottom()` ordering as a potential "race"; it isn't. Both calls run sequentially in the same `requestAnimationFrame` callback (`useTerminal.ts:86-107`) on a single thread — there is no scheduling boundary between them. Treating this as a race is exaggerated severity.
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  d1f71532-ef0c-4e0e-9f5b-7eb743bba38a:
    id: d1f71532-ef0c-4e0e-9f5b-7eb743bba38a
    content: 'Reviewer suggested locking ordering with `mock.invocationCallOrder` — this would assert an internal call sequence rather than observable behavior. Functionally the order is irrelevant: `refresh(0, rows-1)` repaints from current buffer state and `scrollToBottom()` sets `viewport.ydisp = ybase` regardless of whether `fit()` resized first; the only observable outcome is "viewport is at bottom after reveal" which the existing tests already cover. Adding the ordering test would couple to implementation detail without protecting any user-visible behavior.'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
