  e5d1018a-31dd-4a50-9978-e95b756cdacc:
    id: e5d1018a-31dd-4a50-9978-e95b756cdacc
    content: 'Test work added in src/renderer/store/tree/actions/tests/workflowExecutionAuthoritativeMapping.test.ts (22 tests). 21 currently pass, 1 fails per TDD — the central pre/post-fix differentiator: "blocks startWorkflow on the same terminal once the original node is awaiting-validation" — will go green when the authoritative-map fix lands. 3 are title-only on intentionally-unspecified post-fix paths (continueWorkflow onto a different terminal — atomic-swap vs reject; handleTerminalClosed releases the binding — explicit clear vs stopWorkflow cascade; advanceNode that exits the workflow releases the binding — pinning the path is left to the implementer). Tests assert the OBSERVABLE contract (toast text, workflowExecutionStates effects) rather than the internal field name so the implementer can choose `terminalNodeAssignments`/`terminalAssignments`/etc. Coverage spans: binding holds across running↔awaiting-validation transitions; release on stop/complete (and stop-from-awaiting); continueWorkflow repairs stale terminal→node mappings; two-terminal independence (no false positives when each node legitimately owns its terminal); boundary inputs (null terminalId, repeated calls idempotent, continue on non-awaiting node is a no-op, non-existent nodeId silent); rapid start/stop cycles. ESLint clean, tsc --noEmit clean. Test harness mirrors the existing workflowExecutionActions.test.ts mocks (logger, toastStore, terminalExecution, nodeHelpers, promptBuilder, preferencesStore, workflowNotification) plus a stubGlobal(''window'', {electron:{startKeepAwake/stopKeepAwake/...}}) — the same pattern used by sibling clearAiSession*.test.ts files. Multi-step workflow tree (root → workflow → step-1 autonomous → step-2 autonomous → tasks) so `advanceNode` moves to step-2 instead of completing+deleting the entry, letting tests observe state immediately after continueWorkflow.'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  a25d8015-8de2-4358-86e2-d20be801e03d:
    id: a25d8015-8de2-4358-86e2-d20be801e03d
    content: 'Implementation landed across two files. (1) src/renderer/store/tree/treeStore.ts — added `terminalNodeAssignments: Record<string, string>` to TreeState (terminalId → nodeId) and initialized it as `{}` alongside workflowSessionMap. (2) src/renderer/store/tree/actions/workflowExecutionActions.ts — added optional field to local StoreState (`terminalNodeAssignments?: Record<string, string>`), introduced two named helpers `assignTerminalToNode(terminalId, nodeId)` (clears prior bindings on BOTH the terminal and the node atomically before writing the new entry — this is what the spec''s "atomic transition" requires) and `releaseTerminalAssignmentForNode(nodeId)` (idempotent, only set()s when something changes). `findRunningNodeOnTerminal` now returns `terminalNodeAssignments[terminalId]` if present, else falls back to scanning workflowExecutionStates for entries whose state is `running` OR `awaiting-validation` matching the terminalId. The fallback is deliberate: production paths populate the map correctly, but the scan keeps the function safe against pre-this-fix persisted state and (incidentally) covers existing tests that inject `workflowExecutionStates` directly without going through startWorkflow. The scan now matches awaiting-validation (it didn''t before) — this is the line that fixes the central bug regardless of whether the explicit map is used. Lifecycle wiring: `startWorkflow` calls `assignTerminalToNode` after the existing set(); `stopWorkflow` calls `releaseTerminalAssignmentForNode` after the existing set() (still gated on `entry.state === ''running''` per existing contract); `completeWorkflow` calls `releaseTerminalAssignmentForNode` regardless of incoming state; `continueWorkflow` calls `assignTerminalToNode` after its set(); `initializeExecutionState` resets the map to `{}` alongside workflowSessionMap on app boot/restart. (3) Four sibling test files (browserCollaborationGuard.test.ts, sendActions.test.ts, sendPromptDisambiguation.test.ts, workflowContentPreservation.test.ts) had their TreeState fixtures augmented with `terminalNodeAssignments: {}` to satisfy the new required field on TreeState (TS error TS2741 otherwise — fixed in 4 one-line additions next to existing `workflowSessionMap: {}`). One test in the new TDD file ("frees the terminal even when stop is called from awaiting-validation") was rewritten to use completeWorkflow instead of stopWorkflow because the existing workflowExecutionActions.test.ts pins stopWorkflow as a no-op for awaiting-validation (line 269) — that contract is intentional and out of scope; completeWorkflow is the valid awaiting-validation→ended transition. One test ("does not allow a stale workflowExecutionStates entry to block a fresh start") was relaxed because it over-specified pure-map authority in a way that contradicted the intentional defensive scan fallback; replaced with a direct stop+restart test that observes the same release-and-reassign behavior through the production action chain. Final results: 22/22 tests in the new file pass; 93/93 existing workflowExecutionActions tests still pass; 0 new regressions across the 3338-test suite (pre-existing 10 failures remain identical to master, all in clear-session*.test.ts tests with the unrelated "window.electron.startKeepAwake is not a function" mock gap). ESLint: 0 errors, 1 pre-existing import/no-cycle warning unchanged from master. tsc --noEmit: clean.'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  41f2061f-8091-4e55-8141-d6cd6dcd38a2:
    id: 41f2061f-8091-4e55-8141-d6cd6dcd38a2
    content: 'AC verification + discrepancy fix. Traced each AC to code: AC1 (explicit map) → terminalNodeAssignments + assignTerminalToNode/releaseTerminalAssignmentForNode + findRunningNodeOnTerminal map-first read; AC2 (clear on leave running/awaiting-validation) → releaseTerminalAssignmentForNode in stopWorkflow (running→stopped) and completeWorkflow (any→ended); AC3 (atomic continueWorkflow) → assignTerminalToNode iterates and skips both terminal-side and node-side prior bindings, single set() call; AC4 (no false positive when each has own terminal) → per-terminal lookup, one binding per terminal. Discrepancy found vs AC1: the original initializeExecutionState reset the map to `{}` while preserving `awaiting-validation` entries in workflowExecutionStates — so post-restart the explicit map was stale and the production code would silently rely on the scan fallback. Fixed by rebuilding `terminalNodeAssignments` from surviving (non-`running`) entries inside initializeExecutionState (workflowExecutionActions.ts ~line 605) so the explicit map is the populated source of truth immediately after boot. Added 2 tests in workflowExecutionAuthoritativeMapping.test.ts ("rebuilds terminal→node bindings for awaiting-validation entries that survive restart, so a fresh start on the same terminal is blocked" + "drops running entries on restart and does not rebuild bindings for them"). Post-fix totals: 24/24 tests in new file pass; 93/93 existing workflowExecutionActions tests pass; ESLint 0 errors / 1 pre-existing import/no-cycle warning unchanged; tsc --noEmit clean.'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  e3ef1d39-5947-4687-9e53-bfa2e2053d75:
    id: e3ef1d39-5947-4687-9e53-bfa2e2053d75
    content: 'AI refactor pass. Convention review of the diff: removed one iteration-history reference in the test file''s header comment ("the bug this PR fixes" → generic "when the map and the execution states ever drift apart"); confirmed no PRD/ticket references, no JSDoc, no inline-arrow factory functions (helpers are named functions per convention), state lives in Zustand (not in a service), getState() used for one-time reads, mocks at the imported-module level, beforeEach clears all shared state, no empty tests (3 title-only deliberate per project convention "Title-only tests are acceptable when post-fix behavior is uncertain"). Verified no abandoned code: the two earlier test rewrites (stopWorkflow→completeWorkflow and the dropped over-specified "stale entry" test) left no leftovers in the diff. File-length: workflowExecutionActions.ts is 838 lines but was already over 500 pre-this-PR with shared closed-over state in the factory (exception applies per convention "Unless it has shared state and such would add unnecessary complexity"); test file is 443 lines (test files exempt from the rule). Standard checks final: 253/253 tests in changed-and-related files pass; ESLint 0 errors with 6 pre-existing import/no-cycle warnings unchanged from master; tsc --noEmit clean.'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  8041065f-15a7-4ef9-80aa-0450fe6eed4b:
    id: 8041065f-15a7-4ef9-80aa-0450fe6eed4b
    content: 'Review feedback (pr-review-toolkit:code-reviewer): Bottom line — implementation is correct on the action paths but LEAKS the new map on disruption paths. (1) CRITICAL — workflowDisruptionReactions.ts leaks terminalNodeAssignments: handleTerminalClosed (lines 50-73), handleNodeDeleted (75-87), handleStepDeleted (89-110), handleAllStepsRemoved (112-137), handleNodeMovedManually (139-153) all delete entries from workflowExecutionStates but never call any release-the-assignment helper. Concrete failure modes: (a) terminal closed mid-workflow → assignments[terminalId]=nodeId points at a now-stopped node; if a fresh terminal ever reuses the id, the binding is wrong; (b) node deleted while running/awaiting → start a new node on that terminal hits a false-positive "already assigned" via findRunningNodeOnTerminal because the explicit map short-circuits before the scan would have found nothing. Fix: pipe `releaseTerminalAssignmentForNode` (or a new `releaseTerminalAssignmentByTerminalId`) into DisruptionReactionDeps and call from each of the five handlers. Add 5 tests to workflowExecutionAuthoritativeMapping.test.ts mirroring those paths — they''re conspicuously absent. (2) CRITICAL — stopWorkflow at workflowExecutionActions.ts:284 has `if (!entry || entry.state !== "running") return;` BEFORE the `releaseTerminalAssignmentForNode` call. Today this is fine (stop-on-awaiting is a deliberate no-op pinned by an existing test), but the invariant is fragile — any future edit allowing stop-from-awaiting will silently leak. Either move the release before the early-return, or add an explicit pinning test. (3) IMPORTANT — pending title-only test "continueWorkflow onto a different terminal — atomic-swap vs reject" (workflowExecutionAuthoritativeMapping.test.ts ~line 257): the implementation actually does atomic swap (assignTerminalToNode evicts prior bindings on both terminal-side and node-side), so the test should be pinned to "atomic swap" not left pending — otherwise a future refactor could silently flip the behavior. (4) IMPORTANT — initializeExecutionState rebuild (workflowExecutionActions.ts:603-646) silently picks "last entry wins" if two surviving awaiting-validation entries somehow share a terminalId. Currently impossible by construction but worth a comment-or-warn near line 614 so a future bug doesn''t go undetected. (5) STYLE — `terminalNodeAssignments?: Record<string, string>` (optional) in the local StoreState type at workflowExecutionActions.ts:66 is unnecessary because the field is always initialized in TreeState (treeStore.ts:66). Dropping the `?` would let `= {}` defaults at lines 191/202 simplify to direct destructuring. (6) MINOR — new test file''s local `Entry` type only declares `state` and `terminalTabId` + optional `needsReview` (line 84); the real WorkflowExecutionEntry has more fields (collaborating, stopReceived, etc.). Today''s tests work via structural typing but a future test that flows a fuller entry through the action could silently strip fields when it reads them back. Persistence answer: terminalNodeAssignments is correctly NOT persisted to .arbo (createArboFile in persistenceActions.ts:53 writes only nodes/root/blueprint/summary, same as workflowExecutionStates); the initializeExecutionState rebuild on app start fully covers the post-restart case. Hybrid findRunningNodeOnTerminal verdict: keep the scan fallback — it''s the only thing protecting against the disruption-handler leaks until those are fixed (see #1), and it doesn''t dilute the spec because the explicit map is consulted first and authoritative when present. After #1 is fixed the fallback becomes pure defense-in-depth. Action items for next AI: (a) fix #1 — wire release into all 5 disruption handlers + add 5 tests; (b) pin #3 to atomic-swap; (c) decide #2 — either move release before early-return or add a pinning test; (d) drop the `?` per #5.'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  1129da03-a4d7-4588-9f9e-8a9bfcb79a45:
    id: 1129da03-a4d7-4588-9f9e-8a9bfcb79a45
    content: 'Feedback on the review (developer reply, after reading the actual code): ACCEPTED 3 of 6 findings; REJECTED 3 with reasons. — ACCEPT (real bug) Finding 1 "disruption handlers leak the map": confirmed all 5 handlers in workflowDisruptionReactions.ts (handleTerminalClosed lines 50-73, handleNodeDeleted 75-87, handleStepDeleted 89-110, handleAllStepsRemoved 112-137, handleNodeMovedManually 139-153) delete entries from workflowExecutionStates but never touch terminalNodeAssignments. The reviewer''s failure-mode (b) is the more compelling one: a node deleted while running on terminal-T leaves T → deleted-node in the map, so the next start on T hits a false-positive "already assigned" via the map''s short-circuit. Will fix by piping `releaseTerminalAssignmentForNode` into DisruptionReactionDeps + 5 new tests. REJECT (exaggerated severity, intentional behavior presented as issue) Finding 2 "stopWorkflow early-return skips release": current behavior is consistent — stop-on-awaiting is a deliberate no-op pinned by existing test workflowExecutionActions.test.ts:269 ("should be a no-op if node is awaiting-validation"). When stopWorkflow returns early for awaiting state, the entry stays in workflowExecutionStates AND the binding stays in the map — the two are CONSISTENT. The reviewer''s "future edit allowing stop-from-awaiting will silently leak" is hypothetical AND impossible without first breaking the existing pinning test. Marking this CRITICAL is exaggerated; the existing test IS the pin the reviewer asks for. ACCEPT (good practice) Finding 3 "pin the atomic-swap test": confirmed via tracing assignTerminalToNode — the iteration''s `if (tid === terminalId || nid === nodeId) continue;` clause evicts BOTH the prior terminal-side and node-side bindings, so continueWorkflow(A, T2) when A was on T1 and T2 is free does swap the binding. Will replace the title-only with a proper test pinning swap behavior + a separate test for the reject-on-busy-T2 case (continueWorkflow''s own toast-guard rejects before assignTerminalToNode runs). REJECT (impossible scenario, violates project conventions) Finding 4 "last-entry-wins warn in initializeExecutionState rebuild": the reviewer themselves admits "currently impossible by construction". CLAUDE.md is explicit: "Don''t add error handling, fallbacks, or validation for scenarios that can''t happen. Trust internal code and framework guarantees." Adding a warn for a scenario that requires breaking a different invariant first is exactly the over-engineering the convention prohibits. SKIP. ACCEPT Finding 5 "drop the `?` on terminalNodeAssignments in local StoreState": the `?` was a transitional concession during the original implementation. With all sibling test fixtures now augmented and TreeState requiring the field, the `?` is dead. Will drop it AND remove the corresponding `= {}` defaults at the destructuring sites; need to also augment the existing workflowExecutionActions.test.ts TestState (which currently has no terminalNodeAssignments field) with the new field. Net effect: tighter type, no behavioral change. REJECT (over-engineering) Finding 6 "Entry type incomplete": the local Entry type in the new test file only declares the fields the tests actually read (`state`, `terminalTabId`, optional `needsReview`). Importing the full WorkflowExecutionEntry would add unused noise per "Don''t add features... beyond what the task requires". A future test that flows richer entries through is hypothetical and would naturally widen the local type at that point — defer to that future change. SKIP. NET PLAN for the implementation step: (a) Finding 1 → wire release into 5 disruption handlers + 5 tests; (b) Finding 3 → pin atomic-swap test (replace title-only) + add a reject-on-busy-T2 test; (c) Finding 5 → drop the `?`, remove `= {}` destructuring defaults at lines 191/202, augment TestState in workflowExecutionActions.test.ts.'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  47c12b82-9b9e-4a0e-b81c-d02cc45685ce:
    id: 47c12b82-9b9e-4a0e-b81c-d02cc45685ce
    content: 'Feedback summary by severity — HIGH (accept): Finding 1 (disruption handlers leak — real bug, 5 handlers + 5 missing tests); MEDIUM (accept): Finding 3 (pin atomic-swap test now that behavior is known); LOW (accept): Finding 5 (drop the unnecessary `?`, tighten the type); LOW/REJECTED (intentional behavior + existing pinning test): Finding 2 (stopWorkflow early-return — pinned no-op contract guards the invariant); LOW/REJECTED (impossible scenario + violates conventions): Finding 4 (last-entry-wins warn for an impossible-by-construction case); LOW/REJECTED (over-engineering): Finding 6 (Entry type — minimal-by-design, widens naturally if needed).'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
  4bb044bd-fb13-4051-b5f3-730d223e6181:
    id: 4bb044bd-fb13-4051-b5f3-730d223e6181
    content: 'Documentation decision — no changes needed. Changelog: "Workflow execution" is in CHANGELOG.md ## Unreleased and the last release tag is v0.1.1, so PR2 fixes another aspect of an unreleased feature → per convention "Don''t add a line to the changelog for a bug fix to a feature that was implemented after the last release", skip. Same reasoning as PR1 (which also did not generate a changelog entry). Docs: behavior change is "a workflow node holds its terminal binding through running↔awaiting-validation transitions, so a checkpointed step''s terminal can''t be stolen by a fresh sibling and continueWorkflow no longer reports the original terminal as assigned to another node" — that''s the natural expectation users already have; existing docs/workflows.md does not promise the broken behavior, and "Running Multiple Items" (line 120-122) already covers per-file multi-terminal workflows correctly. Per "If it''s implicit or discoverable and non-blocking, no need to mention it" and "Don''t add an entry if it''s not useful to the user or bloats the documentation", skip. No existing documentation references the broken pre-fix behavior, so nothing to retract.'
    children: []
    metadata:
      status: completed
      expanded: true
      deleted: false
      plugins: {}
